Subject: A baseline lowering tier that refuses nothing

What this does
  A baseline lowering tier that refuses nothing.

Why we need it
  A valid function can still be refused: a 5,000-case `switch` exceeds the liveness cap, a long `if` chain exceeds the CFG state cap, a 4,500-argument call site or a 5,000-parameter function exceeds the 4,096-register operand range, and a function with more than 65,535 property sites cannot name a PIC site. A register build has no stack interpreter to fall back to, so every one of those is a valid program the build rejects.

What it adds
  A third tier, entered whenever the optimising path defers: its value identity is the operand-stack depth, so it needs no liveness, no SSA (hence no settle, no phi), no promotion, no destructive reuse, no folding and no fusing -- the caps that bound those analyses do not exist. Values below the 16-bit register range are their own register; a deeper one lives in the top of the register file and is reached through two new opcodes, `ROP_slot_get r,u` / `ROP_slot_put u,r`, planted by a post-pass (`regvm_baseline_spill`) that inlines the stack shapers as real parallel copies and remaps `pc_to_insn`. The call window, the argument packing (`regvm_build_arg_array`), the ownership replay and the encoder are shared with the optimising path; the hot store handler's "does this instruction have a site" test is `f->pic != NULL` -- the same question `site != REG_IC_NONE` asks, with no second immediate compare.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes. Regenerated register blobs are in `9999`.
